---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import ResourceEntry from "../../components/ResourceEntry.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Data | Miguel González Calvo"
  description="Primary-source economic datasets on the two great American paper-money episodes: the Continental dollar (1777–1781) and the Civil War greenbacks (1859–1866)."
>
  <main class="page">
    <h1 class="page-title">Data</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">
      Primary-source economic data, transcribed from the original printed tables
      into machine-readable form and released openly. Both datasets underlie
      papers of mine, but they are published separately because the
      transcription is useful independently of the argument it was built for.
    </p>

    <h2 class="section__title">Datasets</h2>
    <div class="rowlist section">
      <ResourceEntry
        title="The Continental dollar, 1777–1781"
        description="Scales of depreciation enacted by seven states to settle debts in Continental currency, with contemporary merchants' quotations for Philadelphia and Virginia."
        url="/data/continental-dollar"
      />
      <ResourceEntry
        title="The greenbacks, 1859–1866"
        description="Gold prices, commodity prices, wages and the money stock during the American Civil War, from Wesley C. Mitchell's History of the Greenbacks."
        url="/data/greenbacks"
      />
    </div>

    <h2 class="section__title">Method</h2>
    <div class="doc">
      <p>
        Everything here is transcribed <strong
          >from page images rather than OCR</strong
        >. For eighteenth- and nineteenth-century tables this matters more than
        it sounds: optical character recognition corrupts the fractions that
        pervade the originals and confuses digits in ways that change values
        materially. In one case the difference between an OCR reading and the
        page was 800 against 300.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where a second source reproduces the same series, the two are
        cross-checked and the agreement rate is reported. Where sources
        conflict, both are published rather than one being silently preferred —
        and where the enacted statute survives, it is used in preference to any
        later compilation.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every CSV carries a comment header giving the source, table and page
        numbers, so any figure can be traced back to the printed original
        without consulting the README. Transcriptions are released under CC0;
        please cite the underlying sources as well, which are listed in each
        repository.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you find an error in a transcription, please open an issue. I would
        rather be corrected than cited wrongly.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
